                    <note type="editorial"> 
    A solicitor involved in attempting to sort out Godwin's financial affairs in 1824 and 1825 at the point of Godwin's financial tribulkations and bankrupcy. 
    There may be some confusion with <persName key="KEA03">Edmund Kean</persName>, but the visits in this period to the solicitor are intense and, 
    while Godwin varies as to whether he writes Kean or Keane, the context for many entries (often associated with Booth who is helping Godwin), means that the attribution is reasonably certain.
